  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Golovinomyces latisporus is a fungal pathogen belonging to the powdery mildew family that affects various plant hosts in New England. It creates a characteristic white, powdery coating on plant leaves and stems, compromising plant health and aesthetics.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Powdery White Coating",
      "description": "Distinctive white to grayish powdery fungal growth visible on leaf surfaces, stems, and buds",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Structure",
      "description": "Produces broadly ellipsoid conidia (spores) that are notably wide relative to their length, giving the species its 'latisporus' name meaning 'broad-spored'",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Host Range",
      "description": "Commonly infects ornamental and wild plants in the region, with preference for certain plant families",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Golovinomyces latisporus thrives on plant leaves in New England's cool, humid conditions, favoring shaded areas and plants with poor air circulation.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Powdery mildews like G. latisporus are unique fungi that can survive on living plants without killing them, allowing the pathogen to spread throughout an entire growing season.",
